1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,232
Python lists are not arrays.

2
00:00:02,232 --> 00:00:02,854
Here's what's

3
00:00:02,854 --> 00:00:04,213
really in memory.

4
00:00:04,213 --> 00:00:04,930
A list can mix

5
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:06,302
types, like this.

6
00:00:06,302 --> 00:00:07,249
That's because a list

7
00:00:07,249 --> 00:00:08,575
doesn't store the values.

8
00:00:08,575 --> 00:00:09,741
It stores references:

9
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:10,709
arrows to objects

10
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:12,698
somewhere else in memory.

11
00:00:12,848 --> 00:00:13,811
Each object is a

12
00:00:13,811 --> 00:00:14,849
separate box, with

13
00:00:14,849 --> 00:00:16,437
its own overhead.

14
00:00:16,487 --> 00:00:17,497
An array stores the

15
00:00:17,497 --> 00:00:18,841
raw values themselves,

16
00:00:18,841 --> 00:00:20,634
packed side by side.

17
00:00:20,684 --> 00:00:22,130
Every item has the same

18
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:23,850
type, and the same size.

19
00:00:23,850 --> 00:00:25,737
Here, four bytes each.

20
00:00:25,787 --> 00:00:27,179
So the computer finds any

21
00:00:27,179 --> 00:00:28,669
item with simple maths:

22
00:00:28,669 --> 00:00:29,405
start, plus

23
00:00:29,405 --> 00:00:31,167
index, times size.

24
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:32,542
Recap.

25
00:00:32,542 --> 00:00:33,700
Lists hold references,

26
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:34,982
so they're flexible.

27
00:00:34,982 --> 00:00:36,560
Arrays hold raw values, so

28
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:38,658
they're compact and fast.

29
00:00:39,008 --> 00:00:40,167
Next up: Python's

30
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:41,279
array module.

31
00:00:41,279 --> 00:00:43,216
Follow for the whole series.
